[[File:Benvenuto Tisi called Il Garofalo - Treasure Room Fresco - Google Art Project.jpg|upright=1.3|thumb|Ceiling of the Treasure Room of the [[National Archaeological Museum of Ferrara |Archaeological Museum of Ferrara]], [[Italy]], painted in 1503–1506]]

{{langnf|fr|'''Trompe-l'œil'''|deceive the eye|paren=left}}; {{IPAc-en|t|r|ɒ|m|p|ˈ|l|ɔɪ}} {{respell|tromp|-|LOY}}; {{IPA|fr|tʁɔ̃p lœj|lang|Fr-Paris--trompe-l’œil.ogg}}) is an artistic technique that creates a highly realistic [[optical illusion]] of [[three-dimensional space]] and objects on a [[Two-dimensional space|two-dimensional]] surface. {{lang|fr|Trompe-l'œil}}, which is most often associated with painting, tricks the viewer into perceiving painted objects or spaces as real. [[Forced perspective]] is a related illusion in architecture, and [[Op art]] is a modern style mostly dealing with geometric patterns. ''Trompe-l'œil'' has also been used as a term for analogous illusions and deceptions in [[literature]], including those which create a frustrated interpretive expectation or mirror a character's experience through literary structure.<ref name=":1">{{Cite journal |last=Belsey |first=Catherine |date=1995 |title=Love as Trompe-l'oeil: Taxonomies of Desire in Venus and Adonis |url=https://academic.oup.com/sq/article/46/3/257-276/5084804 |journal=Shakespeare Quarterly |volume=46 |issue=3 |pages=257–276 |doi=10.2307/2871118|url-access=subscription }}</ref><ref name=":2">{{Cite book |last=Acciaioli |first=Stefania |url=https://books.fupress.com/isbn/9788866552253 |title=Il "trompe-l'œil" letterario, ovvero il sorriso ironico nell'opera di Wilhelm Hauff |date=2012 |publisher=Firenze University Press |isbn=978-88-6655-224-6 |edition=1 |series=Premio Tesi di Dottorato |volume=24 |location=Firenze |language=it |doi=10.36253/978-88-6655-225-3}}</ref>

